mm/mprotect: a writable batch of a shared mapping's PTEs is of PTEs alike in dirtiness

change_pte_range batches a large folio's PTEs alike in their write and
soft-dirty bits, not their dirty bits; modify_prot_start_ptes collects
the batch's dirty bits into its first PTE, and making a shared mapping
writable again (MM_CP_TRY_CHANGE_WRITABLE) makes the whole batch
writable if that one is dirty (can_change_shared_pte_writable). One PTE
stored to makes its folio's clean PTEs writable too, which a filesystem
that needs a write fault for each page may not have had: fxdax's DAX
pages are made the VM's own one at a time, at their write faults, and a
store through a clean PTE so made writable went to a page the host had
not given the VM, which refused it until the VM went down (fxdax
TASK-149.42). With MEM_SOFT_DIRTY unset, as the guest's is, soft-dirty
splits no batch. A batch made writable takes PTEs alike in dirtiness
too, so that each is decided as it was before 6.17's batching.

diff --git a/mm/mprotect.c b/mm/mprotect.c
--- a/mm/mprotect.c
+++ b/mm/mprotect.c
@@ -298,7 +298,7 @@
 		nr_ptes = 1;
 		oldpte = ptep_get(pte);
 		if (pte_present(oldpte)) {
-			const fpb_t flags = FPB_RESPECT_SOFT_DIRTY | FPB_RESPECT_WRITE;
+			fpb_t flags = FPB_RESPECT_SOFT_DIRTY | FPB_RESPECT_WRITE;
 			int max_nr_ptes = (end - addr) >> PAGE_SHIFT;
 			struct folio *folio = NULL;
 			struct page *page;
@@ -323,6 +323,17 @@
 				}
 			}
 
+			/*
+			 * A shared mapping's PTE is made writable here if it is
+			 * dirty (can_change_shared_pte_writable), which the batch's
+			 * first stands for once modify_prot_start_ptes collects
+			 * their dirty bits into it: a batch takes PTEs alike in
+			 * that too, so that a clean one, whose page the filesystem
+			 * may yet need a write fault for, stays write-protected.
+			 */
+			if ((cp_flags & MM_CP_TRY_CHANGE_WRITABLE) &&
+			    (vma->vm_flags & VM_SHARED))
+				flags |= FPB_RESPECT_DIRTY;
 			nr_ptes = mprotect_folio_pte_batch(folio, pte, oldpte, max_nr_ptes, flags);
 
 			oldpte = modify_prot_start_ptes(vma, addr, pte, nr_ptes);
